fm — cheatsheet

One pane, one directory. The cursor is remembered per directory by the
name under it, so coming back lands on that file, and a rebuild — a
hidden toggle, a file made or gone — keeps it. Everything else is one
of four sources — the selection, the file under the cursor, the clip,
or cur, the row as the shell beside you reads it — and every key takes
exactly one of them.

move
  h            up a directory
  enter        into it, or open the file — right does both too
  j k          step (arrows too)
  g G          first file, last file
  pgup pgdn    page
  z            zoxide query
  '            mark here
  tab          swap the mark and here; unmarked, the last directory
  t            tag this directory
  `            the tagged ones
  ~            home

Tags are symlinks under ~/.config/fm/tagged. Browsing there, whatever a
key spawns runs in the link's target, not in the tag directory — so ;,
v and the finders on a tag land in the project it points at.

find
  /      search names, substring, case-insensitive
  n N    next, prev — wraps
  f      by name, fuzzy, over the tree
  s      by content, fuzzy
  m      by memory — what was said out loud, newest first, done again
  .      show hidden, . and .. among them

f and s select and act on nothing: they put the cursor on what you
picked, and the verb is the next key — enter, e, o, y. A content hit
keeps its line, so e opens there.

m acts. A log line is a verb and then an address — the command that made
it — so picking one says it again: m runs the verb on the address, from
where fm already stands. The address is whole, so the verb carries its
own place and the pane does not follow it; the pick is the act, not the
trip. Only a c line moves the cursor — c was a select, and a select done
again is the landing — so pick one of those when going there was the
point. Only c writes that log, so m finds what was said out loud, and b
is how a row you merely browsed gets in.

. and .. are entries in the directory like the rest — ls skipped the
two by their leading dot, and the hidden file is that accident. Under
the cursor they make a directory a target like any other: y its path,
c or x it whole, * or = it. On .. enter is h; on . it stays. d refuses
both, as rm(1) does. A first visit lands past them.

act
  space    select
  c x      copy, cut — both fill the clip and clear the selection
  p        paste the clip here, asking a name on a clash
  l        symlink the clip here
  u        drop the clip
  d        delete, after a y
  r        rename
  i I      new file, new directory
  b        bookmark — say the row out loud, into the log m walks
  *        toggle +x
  y Y      yank the path, yank the contents
  = +      clone under ~/Unsynced and go there; + for a dir's contents

  selection    c x d
  cursor       enter e o b r y * = + F
  the clip     p l

The header carries all three — [n] selected, cp:n or cut:n pending,
mark:name. A cd clears the selection. Nothing clears the clip but u, a
link, or a cut that finished moving.

the pronoun
  fm with no argument opens on cur: the directory it names, landed on
  its own name. From then on the row under the cursor is cur, so o, e,
  p and a at the prompt beside you mean this file without being told
  which — and `{c} hands it to anything else. A file made, renamed or
  pasted is landed on, so cur follows what a verb made.

  Only the pane you are looking at says it. A background fm — scrolled,
  or woken by a resize — would take the shell's this, so it keeps quiet
  until the pane has the eye and says its row when it gets it back. The
  log m walks is c's alone: what was said out loud, not every j.

the shell — both directions
  o     from a prompt: open the directory you are in, in a pane
        beside you
  ;     from fm: a shell in a pane beside you, in the directory fm
        is in

q closes the pane and the shell keeps the line you were typing. fm does
not move the shell or type at it.

o is the same word on both sides: the command with no argument takes
the directory you are in, and the key hands it the row — a directory
row is another fm in a pane beside you, a file row its app.

tools — every one a script in sh/, replaceable
  e      edit (e)
  o      open the row in a pane beside you (o)
  w      wipe, at home (w)
  F      format (lint -f)
  f s m  the finders (f, s, m — each piped through fzy)
  b      say the row out loud (c)
  v      version control (v)
  V      gitk
  a      a claude session here (a)

a, o and ; open a pane and fm keeps running; b runs and only flashes.
The rest borrow the screen until they exit; F and w hold it after, so
what they printed survives the redraw.

file
  q    quit
  ?    help, generated from the key table

What a key runs is a name, never a path — bin/ is on $PATH, so any of
them is replaced by writing your own and rebuilding nothing.

change it: keys and help are one table,
fm/4-main/11-bind.go — edit, ./build fm.
